.scratch/
node_modules/
# Build output. `pnpm build` emits every package as real JavaScript, because
# Node can execute neither their `.tsx` nor the `.js` specifiers their `.ts`
# carries — see `packages/core/src/bin.ts` and AGENTS.md's "The build step". It
# is derived from the source beside it, and `pnpm test` rebuilds it, so it never
# belongs in a commit.
packages/*/dist/
# Throwaway prototype fixtures. These mock the design system, so they must
# never reach the remote — ignored rather than merely untracked, so a stray
# `git add -A` cannot pick them up.
prototypes/
.factory/run.lock
.factory/journal.json
# Agent worktrees. Each is a full checkout of this repo that a session working
# another issue created and locked, so committing one would nest the repo inside
# itself. The path rather than `.claude/`, because a project's own agent config
# — settings, subagents, skills — belongs in that directory and is meant to be
# committed.
.claude/worktrees/

# Fixture sites that three test files write here, for two different reasons.
# All three remove them after each run — this entry is so that a run interrupted
# before its cleanup cannot be committed.
#
# `build.test.ts`, for #20's build-twice check: every other site in that file
# goes under `node_modules`, which is ignored already, and this one has to sit
# outside it so that the test runner reloads the site's own modules for the
# second build.
#
# `bin.test.ts`, for #177: Node refuses to strip types from a file under
# `node_modules`, so a site with a `pagedeck.config.ts` that a spawned `pagedeck` must load
# cannot go there at all.
#
# `rollback.build.test.ts`, for #32, on `bin.test.ts`' reason: it builds one
# site twice through the spawned executable and rolls the second build back to
# the first. It writes two directories under this glob — the site, and build 1's
# saved output tree, which is kept beside the site rather than inside it so that
# a rollback cannot restore a copy of itself.
#
# `packages/core` is the nearest directory outside `node_modules` from which
# `@pagedeck/islands` and `react` still resolve, which is what puts all of them here.
# Moving or renaming this entry breaks those files' docblocks.
packages/core/.pagedeck-build-test-*/

# The fixture site `packages/core/src/dev.test.ts` writes (#50), removed after
# the run and ignored so that an interrupted run cannot commit it. Same
# placement and the same reason as the entry above, plus one of its own: Vite's
# watcher ignores `**/node_modules/**`, so an island component module written
# there would never raise the hot update that file is about.
#
# Moving or renaming this entry breaks that file's `SITE` docblock.
packages/core/.pagedeck-dev-test/

# The second fixture site that file writes: a site nothing ever synced, which is
# what a dev server's missing-store failure is asserted against. Its own
# directory rather than a state of the one above, because that site is served by
# a process the whole file shares and deleting its store mid-run would be a
# fixture two tests disagree about.
packages/core/.pagedeck-dev-unsynced/

# The third: a site whose pages render into a layout (#712), served by a dev
# server of its own because the shared one's config declares a content callback.
packages/core/.pagedeck-dev-layout/

# Another: a site whose config imports its root provider by value (#703).
packages/core/.pagedeck-dev-framed/

# The fixture site `packages/core/src/site-modules.test.ts` writes (#703).
packages/core/.pagedeck-site-modules-test/

# The fixture sites `packages/core/src/watch.test.ts` writes (#51) — one per
# spawned watch, since each holds a store whose cursor is what its test is
# about. Removed after each run, and ignored so that a run interrupted before
# its cleanup cannot be committed.
#
# Same placement and the same reason as the two entries above: those tests spawn
# the shipped `dist/bin.js`, and a plain Node process resolves `@pagedeck/core` from
# this repo's `node_modules` while refusing to strip types from a config under
# one.
packages/core/.pagedeck-watch-*/

# The one fixture site `packages/design-system/src/site.build.test.ts` writes
# (#162), for the same reason the entry above exists: the test removes it after
# the run, and this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be
# committed.
#
# `packages/design-system` rather than `packages/core`, because the site's config
# reaches `@pagedeck/design-system/components/*` — bare self-referencing subpaths that
# resolve only from inside this package, and only from outside its
# `node_modules`, where Vitest would hand the config to Node and Node cannot
# transform JSX.
#
# Moving or renaming this entry breaks that file's `SITE` docblock, which argues
# the placement.
packages/design-system/.pagedeck-site-build-test/

# The fixture site `packages/design-system/src/consent-banner.build.test.ts`
# writes (#47), for the reason the entry above exists: the test removes it after
# the run, and this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be
# committed. A name of its own rather than a share of the entry above, because
# the two sites are different sites — that one renders the dogfood site's
# entries, and this one renders the reference consent banner and nothing else,
# so that the emitted bytes its test reads are the banner's.
#
# Same placement and the same two reasons as that entry — the site's config
# reaches `@pagedeck/design-system/components/*`, and a spawned `pagedeck` has to be able to
# load a `pagedeck.config.ts` Node will strip types from.
packages/design-system/.pagedeck-consent-banner-test/

# The fixture site `packages/design-system/src/dev.test.ts` writes (#50), for
# the reason the entry above exists: the test removes it after the run, and this
# is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be committed. A name of
# its own rather than a share of the one above, so that two files of one suite
# can never be writing one directory.
#
# Same placement and the same two reasons as that entry — the site's config
# reaches `@pagedeck/design-system/components/*`, and a spawned `pagedeck` has to be able to
# load a `pagedeck.config.ts` Node will strip types from.
packages/design-system/.pagedeck-dev-test/

# Everything `pnpm check:build-twice` writes
# (`packages/design-system/src/site.build-twice.harness.ts`, #220): the site it
# builds, under `site/`, and the two trees it compares, under `trees/`. Same
# placement and the same reasons as the entry above, and a distinct name so
# that a suite running beside the check does not build into the directory the
# check is comparing. The trees are siblings of the site rather than files left
# inside it, so that build 2 reads exactly the directory build 1 read. That
# harness removes all of it on a green run and **keeps it on a red one**,
# because its report names both trees by path for a reader to open — so this
# entry is also what stops a real determinism failure from being committed as
# two output trees.
packages/design-system/.pagedeck-build-twice-check/

# Everything `pnpm bench:island-scan` writes
# (`packages/design-system/src/island-scan.harness.ts`, #385): the generated
# site it syncs and builds, under `site/`, the probe's timing log, and the
# report. Same placement and the same reasons as the two entries above — the
# site's module map is this package's own catalog, whose specifiers are bare
# self-referencing subpaths that resolve only from inside this package and only
# from outside its `node_modules`. A distinct name so that a run of it beside
# the build-twice check does not sync into the site that check is building. The
# harness clears this whole directory at the start of a run rather than at the
# end — the site and store with the report and the scan log taken on them — so
# what a run leaves behind stays readable until somebody takes a new
# measurement; this entry is what stops any of it from being committed.
packages/design-system/.pagedeck-island-scan/

# The docs site's own build output and store: `packages/docs` is a site as
# well as a package, and `pagedeck build` writes into it. `outDir` is `./site` rather
# than `./dist` precisely so the two trees stay distinguishable — `dist` above
# is what `pnpm build` emits from `src`.
packages/docs/site/
packages/docs/content.db

# The retention store the same builds write (#32, `packages/core/src/retention.ts`):
# every `pagedeck build` keeps its manifest under `<site root>/.pagedeck/manifests/` so that
# a later run can roll back to it. Deliberately outside `outDir` — a store
# inside it would be uploaded to the CDN with the site — which is what puts it
# beside the two entries above rather than under either. `packages/docs`,
# `packages/landing` and `packages/site` are the sites in this repo that are not
# themselves ignored already; every other one a test builds is written under
# `node_modules` or under a `.pagedeck-*` directory this file already names.
packages/docs/.pagedeck/

# The starter copy `add-an-island.build.test.ts` follows the island how-to in
# (#696). The test removes it after each run.
packages/docs/.pagedeck-how-to-island-test-*/

# The landing site's own build output, store and retention store: `packages/landing`
# is a site as well as a package (#189), and `pagedeck build` writes all three into it.
# Three entries rather than a share of the docs site's, because they are two
# different sites — same reasoning as the three above, applied a second time:
# `outDir` is `./site` so the emitted pages stay distinguishable from the `dist`
# that `pnpm build` writes from `src`, and the retention store sits outside
# `outDir` so it is never uploaded with the site.
#
# The `.pagedeck/` entry covers a second file since #336: this site declares
# `build.budget`, and the budget report is now written to `.pagedeck/budget-report.json`
# rather than into `outDir`, for the retention store's reason one clause up — it
# is a CI artifact, and a directory-sync host publishes everything in the output
# tree. No entry of its own, which is the whole argument for the dot-directory:
# one ignored `.pagedeck/` absorbs the next thing a build wants to keep beside a site.
#
# `packages/landing/src/site.build.test.ts` removes all four before and after
# its run — the output tree, the store, the retention store, and the report,
# which became a fourth thing to clean up when #336 moved it out of `outDir`:
# removing the output tree no longer removes it. Four artifacts under three
# entries, because two of them share `.pagedeck/`. The docs site's test removes three
# and not four, since that site declares no budget and so writes no report.
# These entries are so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be
# committed.
packages/landing/site/
packages/landing/content.db
packages/landing/.pagedeck/

# wrangler's own scratch directory beside `packages/landing/wrangler.jsonc` (#52).
packages/landing/.wrangler/

# The same directory beside `packages/docs/wrangler.jsonc` (#6).
packages/docs/.wrangler/

# Everything `pnpm bench:scaling` writes
# (`packages/bench/src/scaling.harness.ts`, #60): one generated site per rung of
# the ladder, each with its own store and output tree, plus the report the run
# leaves for a reader. Placed under this package for the reason the entries
# above give — a spawned `pagedeck` loads the site's `pagedeck.config.ts` in a plain Node
# process, which refuses to strip types under a `node_modules`, and the config
# reaches `@pagedeck/bench/components/*`, bare self-referencing subpaths that resolve
# only from inside this package. A synthetic site at the top of the ladder is
# tens of thousands of generated files, so this entry is also what stops one
# from being committed.
packages/bench/.pagedeck-scaling/

# The one generated site `packages/bench/src/site.build.test.ts` builds (#60),
# for the reason the entries above exist: the test removes it after the run, and
# this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be committed. A
# name of its own rather than a share of the harness's directory below, so that
# a suite running beside `pnpm bench:scaling` can never be building the site the
# ladder is timing.
#
# Same placement and the same two reasons as the design-system entries — the
# site's config reaches `@pagedeck/bench/components/*`, and a spawned `pagedeck` has to be
# able to load a `pagedeck.config.ts` Node will strip types from.
packages/bench/.pagedeck-site-build-test/

# The one fixture site `packages/adapter-netlify/src/site.build.test.ts` writes
# (#270), for the reason every entry above exists: the test removes it after the
# run, and this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be
# committed.
#
# `packages/adapter-netlify` rather than `packages/core`, because the claim that
# file makes is that a redirect a site declared survives a build *and* a
# compile, and the compiler it reads is this package's (#19). Outside its
# `node_modules` because a spawned `pagedeck` loads the site's `pagedeck.config.ts` in a
# plain Node process, which refuses to strip types under a `node_modules`.
packages/adapter-netlify/.pagedeck-site-build-test/

# The fixture site `packages/adapter-netlify/src/removal.build.test.ts` writes
# (#304), for the reason every entry above exists: the test removes it after the
# run, and this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be
# committed.
#
# `packages/adapter-netlify` for `site.build.test.ts`' reason one entry up: the
# claim is that a redirect an incremental build wrote for a page it removed
# survives a compile. Outside its `node_modules` because a spawned `pagedeck` refuses to
# strip types from a `pagedeck.config.ts` there.
packages/adapter-netlify/.pagedeck-removal-build-test/

# The site port's own build output, store and retention store: `packages/site`
# is a site as well as a package (#56), and `pagedeck build` writes all three into it.
# Three entries rather than a share of the two sites above, because they are
# three different sites — same reasoning applied a third time: `outDir` is
# `./site` so the emitted pages stay distinguishable from the `dist` that `pnpm
# build` writes from `src`, and the retention store sits outside `outDir` so it
# is never uploaded with the site.
#
# The `.pagedeck/` entry covers a second file since #336, exactly as the landing site's
# does: this site declares `build.budget`, and the budget report is written to
# `.pagedeck/budget-report.json` rather than into `outDir`.
#
# `packages/site/src/site.build.test.ts` removes all four before and after its
# run — the output tree, the store, the retention store, and the report, a
# fourth artifact since #336 for the reason the landing site's entry gives —
# which is four artifacts under three entries, two of them sharing `.pagedeck/`. The
# docs test removes three, having no budget and so no report. These entries are
# so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be committed.
packages/site/site/
packages/site/content.db
packages/site/.pagedeck/

# Everything `packages/site/src/deploy.build.test.ts` writes (#57), for the
# reason every entry above exists: the test removes all of it after the run, and
# this is so that a run interrupted before its cleanup cannot be committed. Seven
# directories, because a deploy has seven places and they must not be one — the
# fixture site, build 1's saved output tree, the local origin the deploy writes
# into, a copy of that origin the spawned incremental deploy reads a published
# manifest back from, the origin a dry run is pointed at so that "nothing was
# written" is a claim about a path of its own, the staging directory the
# out-of-band edge artifacts are left in, and the origin the spawned *applying*
# run files its own build into (#287), which is its own path so that "this is
# what a first apply leaves, and it is one document" is a claim about a
# directory the other deploys never touched.
#
# The origin is a sibling of the site rather than a directory inside it, on
# `rollback.build.test.ts`' reasoning: inside would put the deployed copy under
# a directory `pagedeck build` walks, and the second build would then be building the
# first deploy back into itself.
#
# `packages/site` rather than `packages/core`, because the site being deployed is
# this package's, and outside its `node_modules` because a spawned `pagedeck` loads
# the site's `pagedeck.config.ts` in a plain Node process, which refuses to strip types
# under a `node_modules`.
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-tree/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-origin/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-origin-2/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-dry-origin/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-staging/
packages/site/.pagedeck-deploy-build-test-publish-origin/

# The scratch site `parity.build.test.ts` builds, and the copy of its output
# tree that file mutilates to prove the harness can fail (#58). Removed before
# and after each run; the entry is so that an interrupted run cannot be
# committed.
#
# A scratch directory rather than `packages/site` itself, which
# `site.build.test.ts` already builds: Vitest runs test files in parallel, so
# two files syncing one store and building one output tree would race. Its
# `pagedeck.config.ts` re-exports `siteConfig()`, so what is built is this site's own
# declaration at another path — and it sits inside `packages/site` and outside
# its `node_modules` for the reason the deploy fixtures above do.
packages/site/.pagedeck-parity-build-test/
packages/site/.pagedeck-parity-build-test-broken/

# The scratch site `fonts.build.test.ts` builds (#45), removed before and
# after each run for the reason every entry above exists: an interrupted run
# must not be committed. Same placement, same `pagedeck.config.ts` that re-exports
# `siteConfig()`, and the same reason as the parity scratch directory above —
# a second file building `packages/site` itself would race
# `site.build.test.ts`.
packages/site/.pagedeck-fonts-build-test/

# The parity report `parity compare --report` writes (#58): the CI job keeps it
# as a build artifact and `docs/dogfood-parity.md` names the same path for a
# local run, so it is a file a reader is invited to produce here. It is derived
# from the build beside it and says nothing a rerun would not say again.
packages/site/parity-report.json

# The scratch site the two audit harnesses build, and the Lighthouse report one
# of them writes (#59). Removed before each run; the entries are so that an
# interrupted run cannot be committed. Two directories rather than one, because
# `pnpm test:a11y-harness` and `pnpm test:lighthouse-harness` are separate commands and
# either may be running while the other is — the same reasoning the parity and
# deploy fixtures above are split on.
#
# The report is derived from the build beside it and says nothing a rerun would
# not say again; `.github/workflows/lighthouse.yml` keeps it as a build artifact
# and `docs/success-criteria.md` names the same path for a local run, so it is a
# file a reader is invited to produce here.
packages/site/.pagedeck-a11y-harness/
packages/site/.pagedeck-site-audit/

# The scratch site `packages/site/src/runtime.build.test.ts` builds (#292), with
# React in a chunk of its own so the framework's own bytes can be read off a
# file. Removed before and after the run; ignored so that an interrupted run
# cannot be committed. A name of its own, for the parity scratch directory's
# reason: a second file building one directory would race the first.
packages/site/.pagedeck-runtime-build-test/

# The fixture site `packages/search/src/multi-shard.build.test.ts` builds (#456)
# — six pages naming 3,840 identifiers, which is a corpus whose index splits
# across shards. Removed before and after each run, for the reason every entry
# above exists: a run interrupted before its cleanup must not be committed, and
# a generated corpus of that size is the last thing to commit by accident.
#
# Inside this package and outside its `node_modules` because a spawned `pagedeck`
# loads the site's `pagedeck.config.ts` in a plain Node process, which refuses to strip
# types under a `node_modules`.
packages/search/.pagedeck-multi-shard-test/

# The fixture site `packages/search/src/site.build.test.ts` builds. Named here
# for the same reason as the entry above, which is the whole of why it is named
# at all: it was the one fixture directory in this repository that no line
# covered, so an interrupted run left it committable while every sibling was
# safe.
packages/search/.pagedeck-locale-index-test/

# The fixture sites `packages/search/src/incremental.build.test.ts` builds, one
# per case, on the entry above's reason: a spawned `pagedeck` has to load their
# configs, and an interrupted run must not leave them committable.
packages/search/.pagedeck-incremental-index-*/

# The CMS example's own build output, store and retention store (#694):
# `packages/cms-example` is a site as well as a package, and its
# `site.build.test.ts` builds it in place.
packages/cms-example/site/
packages/cms-example/content.db
packages/cms-example/.pagedeck/

# The copy of the CMS example `packages/docs/src/connect-a-cms.build.test.ts` builds
# (#697). The test removes it after each run.
packages/cms-example/.pagedeck-how-to-cms-test-*/

# The template copy `packages/create-pagedeck/src/template.build.test.ts` builds
# (#77). The test removes it after each run.
packages/create-pagedeck/.pagedeck-template-build-test-*/
